{% if hardware_mbw_avg is defined %}
### Achieved bandwidth — `mbw -q -n {{ hardware_mbw_runs }} {{ hardware_mbw_size_mb }}`

{% for line in hardware_mbw_avg %}
- {{ line }} MiB/s
{% endfor %}

**This is single-threaded copy bandwidth — it is NOT the ~68 GB/s aggregate figure the plan's
tok/s estimates use.** `mbw` runs one thread, and one Broadwell core cannot saturate four memory
channels. Treat these numbers as a *regression check only*: useful for spotting "something got
worse after a RAM change", useless as an absolute.

To measure the aggregate bandwidth llama.cpp can actually pull with 20 threads, use Intel MLC
(`mlc --max_bandwidth`, which spawns threads across cores) or STREAM built with OpenMP. Expect a
number roughly an order of magnitude above the figures here.
